  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "Mzo4zp2fGztL2yguxE+ZCciDDOc=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:50.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## One service, selected at boot\n\nAuthorization in a Reactor is decided by a **single service selected once at boot** \u2014 not per request, not per resolver, and not per document. Two environment flags pick which service you get:\n\n| Policy | When | What the enforcement helpers do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `OPEN` | `AUTH_ENABLED=false` | Every check passes for everyone, including anonymous callers |\n| `ADMIN_ONLY` | `AUTH_ENABLED=true`, document permissions off | Only addresses in `ADMINS` pass any check |\n| `DOCUMENT_PERMISSIONS` | `DOCUMENT_PERMISSIONS_ENABLED=true` | Full per-document protection plus READ/WRITE/ADMIN grants, with inheritance |\n\n`AUTH_ENABLED=false` bypasses every authorization check, which is why the docs call it development-only.\n\n## The consequence for builders\n\nBecause the policy is resolved centrally, the correct approach in a custom subgraph is to **write one enforcement path** and let it behave correctly under all three policies. A helper call is always safe:\n\n```typescript\nconst handle = await subgraph.assertCanRead(args.identifier, ctx);\nreturn subgraph.reactorClient.get(handle.fetchIdentifier);\n```\n\nUnder `OPEN` that assert is effectively a no-op. Under `ADMIN_ONLY` and `DOCUMENT_PERMISSIONS` it fails closed. Either way the resolver reads the same.\n\nThe rule that falls out of this is stated as a best practice: **write the check unconditionally**. Do not special-case `OPEN` or `ADMIN_ONLY` in a resolver \u2014 the helpers already collapse to the correct behaviour per policy, and a resolver that tries to detect the policy itself is both redundant and a place for the two implementations to drift apart.\n\nThe policy type is exported as `AuthorizationPolicy` from `@powerhousedao/reactor-api` when you need to name it, but needing to name it in a resolver is usually a sign the enforcement path is being written twice.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/03-BuildingUserExperiences/07-Authorization/05-EnforcingAuthorizationInSubgraphs.md and 04-Authorization.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:34:50.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:50.000Z"
    }
  },
